\title{The Knee Is a Quantile:\\
Demand-Multiset Calculus for Budget--Quality Curves,\\
with an Interval-Cover Theory of Deployment Entries}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Truncated-memory inference replaces a system's full record of the past by its $k$
most useful entries. Sweeping $k$ and recording the fraction of positions on which
the truncated system still reproduces the full system's output produces a
\emph{budget--quality curve}; the smallest budget clearing a fixed quality gate $g$
is the \emph{knee} $k^{*}(g)$. On a measured three-domain panel the knee is $16$ on
English prose, $12$ on source code, and---the observation motivating this work---$16$
again on classical mathematical text, whose full-model prediction accuracy is twelve
percentage points lower than prose's ($0.3262$ vs.\ $0.4460$ at context $512$;
$0.3418$ vs.\ $0.4612$ at context $1024$). Doubling the context shifts prose and
mathematics rigidly by $+4$ keys, to $20$.

We develop a \emph{demand-multiset calculus}. A workload is a finite family of
positions carrying a \emph{demand} (the least budget preserving the full system's
output there) and a \emph{correctness bit}. We prove: the knee is the left adjoint of
the agreement curve; the sweep is a function of the demand multiset alone, so equal
demand multisets force equal knees at every gate regardless of accuracy; the joint map
to (knee curve, accuracy) is \emph{surjective}, so no inequality links difficulty to
budget; the knee equals the $\lceil gn\rceil$-th order statistic of the demand
distribution, with an exact tail criterion, a Markov relaxation, and a
perturbation-robustness theorem; the knee is invariant under strictly monotone
reparametrisation of the quality axis and under permutation of positions, shifts
rigidly under translation of the curve, and is trapped between constituent knees under
corpus mixing; and every monotone, bounded, eventually saturating count profile is
realised by an honest workload.

We then formalise the deployment claim ``prose and mathematics share one cache-size
entry; only code shifts'' as an interval point-cover problem: an entry $b$ serves a
knee $k$ at waste tolerance $\delta$ iff $k \le b \le k+\delta$. We prove a
single-entry criterion, a packing lower bound, a greedy covering bound of
$\lfloor (b-a)/(\delta+1)\rfloor + 1$ entries on $[a,b]$, and a min--max duality:
on knees in arithmetic progression of common difference $\delta+1$, packing equals
covering and the minimum entry count is exactly the number of knees. Applied to the
measured knee set $\{12,16\}$ this yields the exact threshold $\delta = 4$---one scale
increment---at which the whole three-domain fleet collapses to the single entry $16$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Consider a sequential predictor maintaining a memory of past positions and attending
to it at each step. Truncation keeps only the $k$ highest-scoring memory entries. Two
observables are natural: \emph{retained agreement} $A(k)$, the fraction of prediction
positions at which the $k$-truncated predictor emits the same output as the
untruncated one; and \emph{accuracy} $\acc$, the fraction of positions at which the
untruncated predictor is correct against the reference text. Fixing a \emph{gate} $g$
we define the \emph{knee} $k^{*}(g) = \min\{k : A(k) \ge g\}$, the cache size a
deployment must provision.

The motivating measurement swept $k$ on a corpus of classical mathematical prose under
a harness byte-identical to one previously run on English prose and on source code.
At context $512$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
budget $k$ & $4$ & $8$ & $12$ & $16$ & $20$ & $24$\\
agreement & $0.907$ & $0.959$ & $0.979$ & $\mathbf{0.987}$ & $0.989$ & $0.988$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent and at context $1024$:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
budget $k$ & $8$ & $12$ & $16$ & $20$ & $24+$\\
agreement & $0.952$ & $0.965$ & $0.978$ & $\mathbf{0.983}$ & pass\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The first passing budget is $16$ at context $512$ and $20$ at context $1024$: exactly
the previously measured prose knees. The full-model accuracies are $0.3262$ and
$0.3418$ for mathematics against $0.4460$ and $0.4612$ for prose---gaps of $0.1198$ and
$0.1194$.

Three predictions were registered in advance: \textbf{P1}, mathematics needs more keys
because of long-range symbolic reference; \textbf{P2}, mathematics sits between prose
and code; \textbf{P3}, mathematics coincides with prose. P1 is refuted, P2 holds only
trivially, P3 is confirmed exactly at both contexts.

\subsection{The question}

Why should a domain twelve points harder to predict need identically many keys? Is
the coincidence an artefact of one corpus, one gate, one mixing ratio---or is it
structural? We argue it is structural. Sections~\ref{sec:calculus}--\ref{sec:laws}
identify what the sweep can and cannot see, culminating in a surjectivity theorem;
Section~\ref{sec:quantile} identifies the knee as an order statistic, giving the
mechanism; Section~\ref{sec:cover} turns the resulting deployment table into a solved
extremal problem.

\section{The demand-multiset calculus}\label{sec:calculus}

\subsection{Curves and knees}

\begin{definition}[Knee]
For a curve $A : \N \to \Q$ and a gate $g \in \Q$, the \emph{knee} is
$k^{*}(A,g) = \inf\{k \in \N : g \le A(k)\}$, with $\inf\emptyset = 0$.
\end{definition}

Two facts hold with no monotonicity assumption: if $g \le A(k)$ for some $k$ then
$g \le A(k^{*}(A,g))$ (attainment), and $g \le A(k)$ implies $k^{*}(A,g) \le k$
(minimality). Contrapositively, $k < k^{*}(A,g)$ implies $A(k) < g$.

\begin{theorem}[Galois adjunction]\label{thm:galois}
Let $A$ be monotone with $g \le A(m)$ for some $m$. Then for all $k$,
\[ k^{*}(A,g) \le k \iff g \le A(k). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) $g \le A(k^{*}) \le A(k)$ by attainment and monotonicity.
($\Leftarrow$) is minimality.
\end{proof}

The knee is thus the left adjoint of the curve. Consequently the knee is monotone in
the gate and antitone in the curve: if $A \le B$ pointwise then
$k^{*}(B,g) \le k^{*}(A,g)$.

\begin{lemma}[Exact determination]\label{lem:exact}
If $g \le A(k)$ and $A(j) < g$ for all $j < k$, then $k^{*}(A,g) = k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Reparametrisation invariance]\label{thm:reparam}
For strictly monotone $\psi : \Q \to \Q$ and any gate $g$,
$k^{*}(\psi \circ A, \psi(g)) = k^{*}(A,g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity gives $\psi(g) \le \psi(A(k)) \iff g \le A(k)$, so both infima are
taken over the same subset of $\N$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:reparam} is the abstract form of the verdict: ``this domain is
harder'' is a distortion of the quality axis, and the knee reads only the order of
curve values, never the values.

\subsection{Workloads}

\begin{definition}[Workload]
A \emph{workload} on $n$ positions consists of a demand function
$r : \{1,\dots,n\} \to \N$ and a correctness function
$c : \{1,\dots,n\} \to \{0,1\}$: $r_i$ is the least budget at which the truncated
predictor reproduces the full predictor's output at position $i$, and $c_i$ records
whether the full predictor is correct there.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Derived observables]
For a workload $D$ on $n > 0$ positions: the \emph{agree count}
$N_D(k) = \#\{i : r_i \le k\}$; the \emph{agreement curve} $A_D(k) = N_D(k)/n$; the
\emph{accuracy} $\acc(D) = \#\{i : c_i = 1\}/n$; and the \emph{demand multiset}
$\mathcal{D}(D) = \{\!\{r_1,\dots,r_n\}\!\}$.
\end{definition}

$N_D$ and $A_D$ are monotone with $0 \le A_D \le 1$, and every gate $g \le 1$ is
reachable: take $k = \sum_i r_i$, which dominates every individual demand.

\section{Invariance: the sweep sees only the demand multiset}

\begin{lemma}
$N_D(k)$ equals the number of elements $\le k$ in $\mathcal{D}(D)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Multiset invariance]\label{thm:multiset}
If two workloads on the same number of positions have equal demand multisets, their
agreement curves are equal at every budget.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[P3, exactly]\label{cor:p3}
Under the same hypothesis $k^{*}(A_D,g) = k^{*}(A_E,g)$ for \emph{every} gate $g$,
with no constraint whatsoever relating $\acc(D)$ and $\acc(E)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Rebitting]
Given a workload $D$ on $n$ positions and $0 \le j \le n$, there is a workload $E$ with
$A_E = A_D$ (hence every knee unchanged) and $\acc(E) = j/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Keep $D$'s demands and set $c_i = [\,i < j\,]$; the number of indices below a
threshold $j \le n$ is exactly $j$.
\end{proof}

\section{Full decoupling}

\begin{definition}[Flat workload]
$\operatorname{flat}(n,k,j)$ has $r_i = k$ for all $i$ and $c_i = [\,i<j\,]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:flat}
For $n>0$, $A_{\operatorname{flat}(n,k,j)}(b) = 0$ for $b<k$ and $=1$ for $b \ge k$.
Hence for every gate $g \in (0,1]$ the knee is exactly $k$; and for $j \le n$ the
accuracy is $j/n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Decoupling / surjectivity]\label{thm:decoupling}
For every $n>0$, every $k \in \N$ and every $j \le n$, there is a workload on $n$
positions whose knee equals $k$ at every gate $g \in (0,1]$ and whose accuracy equals
$j/n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $\operatorname{flat}(n,k,j)$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:flat}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The joint invariant $D \mapsto (k^{*}(A_D,\cdot), \acc(D))$ is onto its natural
codomain, so no inequality of the form ``higher difficulty $\Rightarrow$ larger
budget'' can hold in general. P1 is refuted structurally, not merely empirically.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
There exist workloads $P, M$ on $10^4$ positions with
$k^{*}(A_P,g) = k^{*}(A_M,g) = 16$ for every $g \in (0,1]$ and
$\acc(P) - \acc(M) = 0.1198$: the measured context-$512$ gap with the measured shared
knee.
\end{corollary}

\section{Structural laws of the knee}\label{sec:laws}

\begin{theorem}[Demand domination]\label{thm:domination}
If $r^{D}_i \le r^{E}_i$ for all $i$ and the gate is reachable for $E$, then
$k^{*}(A_D,g) \le k^{*}(A_E,g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Pointwise-smaller demands give a pointwise-larger agree count, hence a
pointwise-larger curve; the knee is antitone in the curve.
\end{proof}

This is the structural content of ``code is cheaper than prose'': code's demand
profile is pointwise below prose's, and the strict gap $12 < 16$ is a measurement on
top of a proved inequality.

\begin{theorem}[Shape preservation]\label{thm:shift}
Let $A$ have reachable gate $g$ with $A(0) < g$, and let $\delta \in \N$. Then
$k^{*}(A(\cdot-\delta), g) = k^{*}(A,g) + \delta$, at every gate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The translated curve attains the gate at $k^{*}(A,g)+\delta$. Below that, either
$b < \delta$, where the translated value is $A(0) < g$, or $b - \delta < k^{*}(A,g)$,
where $A(b-\delta) < g$. Lemma~\ref{lem:exact} concludes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Corpus mixing]\label{thm:mixing}
For monotone $A,B$, weight $\theta \in [0,1]$ and
$M_\theta(k) = \theta A(k) + (1-\theta) B(k)$, with the relevant gates reachable,
\[ \min\bigl(k^{*}(A,g), k^{*}(B,g)\bigr) \le k^{*}(M_\theta, g)
   \le \max\bigl(k^{*}(A,g), k^{*}(B,g)\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Upper: at $k = \max$ both $A(k) \ge g$ and $B(k) \ge g$, so the convex combination is
$\ge g$. Lower: below the min both are $<g$, so the combination is $<g$ (the case
$\theta = 0$ handled separately).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Since the prose and mathematics knees coincide at $16$, every mixture of the two
corpora has knee $16$: the measured number does not depend on the blend ratio.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Markov bridge]\label{thm:markov}
For a workload on $n>0$ positions, if $\sum_i r_i \le (1-g)\,n\,(k+1)$ then
$k^{*}(A_D,g) \le k$; informally $k^{*} \lesssim \bar r/(1-g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each of the $T$ positions with $r_i > k$ contributes at least $k+1$ to $\sum_i r_i$, so
$T(k+1) \le (1-g)n(k+1)$, giving $T \le (1-g)n$. Since $N_D(k)+T = n$ we get
$A_D(k) = (n-T)/n \ge g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realisation]\label{thm:realisation}
Let $t : \N \to \N$ be monotone with $t(k) \le n$ for all $k$ and $t(K)=n$ for some
$K$. Then some workload on $n$ positions has $N_D = t$ exactly, with any prescribed
accuracy $j/n$. If moreover $g n \le t(k)$ and $t(b) < g n$ for all $b<k$, that
workload has knee exactly $k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Define $r_i = \inf\{k : i < t(k)\}$; monotonicity of $t$ gives $r_i \le k \iff i<t(k)$,
and the number of indices below $t(k) \le n$ is $t(k)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The mechanism: the knee is an order statistic}\label{sec:quantile}

\begin{definition}
The $m$-th smallest demand of a workload is $Q_D(m) = \inf\{k : m \le N_D(k)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Quantile identity]\label{thm:quantile}
For a workload on $n>0$ positions and any gate $g$,
$k^{*}(A_D,g) = Q_D(\lceil g n\rceil)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$g \le N_D(k)/n \iff gn \le N_D(k) \iff \lceil gn\rceil \le N_D(k)$, the last step
because $N_D(k)$ is an integer; the two infima are over the same set.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact gate criterion]\label{thm:gate}
With $T_D(k) = \#\{i : r_i > k\}$, $A_D(k) \ge g \iff T_D(k) \le (1-g)n$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:markov} is the one-sided relaxation of Theorem~\ref{thm:gate}. Two
usable directions follow: if every demand is $\le k$ then $k^{*} \le k$ for any gate
$\le 1$; and if $T_D(k) > (1-g)n$ then $k < k^{*}(A_D,g)$ strictly.

\begin{theorem}[Permutation invariance]
Relabelling positions by a bijection leaves every knee unchanged.
\end{theorem}

Together with Corollary~\ref{cor:p3} this says: the sweep is a symmetric function of
the demands alone.

\begin{theorem}[Perturbation robustness]\label{thm:robust}
Let $D,E$ be workloads on $n>0$ positions, $k$ a budget, $g$ a gate. If
$T_D(k) \le (1-g)n$ and the unserved set of $E$ at budget $k$ is contained in that of
$D$, then $k^{*}(A_E,g) \le k$---regardless of how much worse $E$'s accuracy is.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Containment gives $T_E(k) \le T_D(k) \le (1-g)n$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:gate} and
minimality.
\end{proof}

This is the mechanism behind the verdict. Accuracy is a \emph{mean} of the correctness
variable; the knee is a \emph{quantile} of the demand variable. A domain change may
move the first arbitrarily far while leaving the $\lceil gn\rceil$-th order statistic
of the second in place, and Theorem~\ref{thm:robust} quantifies the room: up to
$(1-g)n - T_D(k)$ positions may have their demands perturbed arbitrarily upward. At
$g \approx 0.98$ this is roughly $2\%$ of positions---exactly the regime in which a hard
domain's long-range references live. The only coupling between the two statistics is
that they are computed over the same positions, and Theorem~\ref{thm:decoupling} shows
that coupling carries no information.

\section{Deployment entries as an interval point cover}\label{sec:cover}

\subsection{The measured cells, computed}

Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:realisation} and Lemma~\ref{lem:exact} to the measured step
profiles yields the knees as computations over entire admissible gate windows.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:cells}
For mathematical text at context $512$, $k^{*}(g) = 16$ for every gate
$g \in (0.979,\,0.987]$. At context $1024$, $k^{*}(g) = 20$ for every
$g \in (0.978,\,0.983]$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Mathematics reads as prose]
For every gate in $(0.979,0.987]$ the mathematical-text and prose knees at context
$512$ are the same number $16$, while accuracies differ by exactly
$0.4460 - 0.3262 = 0.1198$. For every gate in $(0.978,0.983]$ both knees at context
$1024$ equal $20$ and accuracies differ by exactly $0.4612 - 0.3418 = 0.1194$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
Mathematical text is strictly harder to predict at both contexts, yet its budget is
not one key larger---it is identical. P1 is refuted on the measured cell.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[One gate certifies both cells]
The admissible windows overlap in $(0.979,0.983]$. On that overlap the context
$512 \to 1024$ increment is exactly $+4$ for mathematical text and exactly $+4$ for
prose, verifying Theorem~\ref{thm:shift} on the measured numbers with a single common
gate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
Composing the mathematical-text sweep with any strictly monotone distortion of the
quality axis, with the gate transported along, still yields knee $16$. Moreover any
$\theta$-mixture of the prose and mathematical-text sweeps at context $512$ has knee
$16$, for every gate in $(0.979,0.987]$ and every $\theta \in [0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

The context-$512$ table is therefore $\text{prose} \mapsto 16$,
$\text{code} \mapsto 12$, $\text{mathematics} \mapsto 16$, with image $\{12,16\}$ of
cardinality $2$.

\subsection{The combinatorial problem}

\begin{definition}[Serving]
An entry $b \in \N$ \emph{serves} a knee $k \in \N$ at waste tolerance $\delta$ iff
$k \le b \le k+\delta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Entry set]
A finite $E \subseteq \N$ is an \emph{entry set} for a knee set $K$ at tolerance
$\delta$ iff every $k \in K$ is served by some $b \in E$.
\end{definition}

Each domain is thus the integer interval $[k,k+\delta]$ and an entry set is a set of
points meeting every interval.

\begin{theorem}[Single-entry criterion]\label{thm:single}
For nonempty finite $K$, a single entry serves all of $K$ iff
$\max K \le \min K + \delta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Serving at $\max K$ and $\min K$ gives $\max K \le b$ and
$b \le \min K + \delta$. ($\Leftarrow$) Take $b = \max K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Separation blocks sharing]\label{lem:sep}
If $k + \delta < \ell$, no entry serves both $k$ and $\ell$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Packing lower bound]\label{thm:packing}
Let $S \subseteq K$ be pairwise $\delta$-separated. Then every entry set $E$ for $K$
satisfies $|E| \ge |S|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose for each $k \in S$ a serving entry $f(k) \in E$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:sep} $f$ is
injective on $S$, so $|S| \le |E|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Greedy entries]
$G(\delta,b,m) = \{\, b - (\delta+1)i : 0 \le i < m \,\}$.
\end{definition}

Anchoring at the top is essential: an entry must never fall below the knee it serves.

\begin{theorem}[Greedy covering bound]\label{thm:greedy}
If every knee of $K$ lies in $[a,b]$, then
$G(\delta, b, \lfloor (b-a)/(\delta+1)\rfloor + 1)$ is an entry set for $K$, of
cardinality at most $\lfloor (b-a)/(\delta+1)\rfloor + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given $k \in [a,b]$ write $y = b-k = q(\delta+1)+s$ with $0 \le s < \delta+1$. The
candidate entry $b - (\delta+1)q = k+s$ lies in the progression because
$q \le \lfloor (b-a)/(\delta+1)\rfloor$, and $k \le k+s \le k+\delta$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Packing meets covering}

\begin{definition}
$\AP(\delta,a,m) = \{\, a + (\delta+1)i : 0 \le i < m \,\}$: $m$ knees spaced exactly
$\delta+1$ apart.
\end{definition}

This set has exactly $m$ elements, is pairwise $\delta$-separated, and lies in
$[a,\, a+(\delta+1)(m-1)]$.

\begin{theorem}[Min--max duality for deployment entries]\label{thm:minmax}
For $\delta, a \in \N$ and $m > 0$,
\[ \min\bigl\{ |E| : E \text{ an entry set for } \AP(\delta,a,m) \bigr\} = m. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Upper bound: apply Theorem~\ref{thm:greedy} with $b = a + (\delta+1)(m-1)$, giving
$\lfloor (\delta+1)(m-1)/(\delta+1)\rfloor + 1 = m$. Lower bound: apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:packing} with $S = K = \AP(\delta,a,m)$, of cardinality $m$ and
$\delta$-separated.
\end{proof}

Packing equals covering on the extremal configuration; neither bound can be improved.

\subsection{The measured fleet}

The measured knee set is $K_{70} = \{12,16\}$, with spread $4$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact entry threshold]
$K_{70}$ admits a single serving entry if and only if $\delta \ge 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:single}: $16 \le 12+\delta \iff \delta \ge 4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $\delta \ge 4$ the entire three-domain fleet---prose, mathematics \emph{and}
code---is served by the single cache size $16$. For $\delta \le 3$ no single entry
suffices and $\{12,16\}$ is an entry set of size $2$, so the minimum is exactly $2$.
\end{corollary}

The operational sentence ``prose and mathematics share one entry; only code shifts''
is precisely the $\delta \le 3$ regime, and the threshold $\delta = 4$ equals the scale
increment---the same $+4$ by which every knee moves when the context doubles.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Knee extraction from a sweep.}
Given a monotone sweep $(k_1,A_1),\dots,(k_m,A_m)$ with $k_1 < \dots < k_m$ and a gate
$g$, return the least $k_j$ with $A_j \ge g$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:galois} binary search
over $j$ is correct: cost $O(\log m)$ comparisons after an $O(m)$ monotone hull pass.
The hull pass matters: measured values may dip within a standard error (e.g.\ $0.988$
at budget $24$ below $0.989$ at budget $20$), and the monotone hull is the principled
repair.

\paragraph{B. Admissible gate window.}
Given the sweep and the reported knee $k_j$, return $(A_{j-1},A_j]$: every gate in it
yields the same knee, by Lemma~\ref{lem:exact}. Reporting the window rather than a
single tuned gate is what makes a knee claim falsifiable. Cost $O(1)$.

\paragraph{C. Minimum deployment entries.}
Given a knee multiset $K$ and tolerance $\delta$: sort descending; repeatedly take the
largest uncovered knee $b$, emit entry $b$, and delete every knee in $[b-\delta,b]$.
Cost $O(|K|\log|K|)$. The emitted entries form a valid cover, while the knees that
triggered emissions are pairwise $\delta$-separated, so Theorem~\ref{thm:packing} shows
no entry set is smaller: the greedy output is exactly optimal for every input, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:minmax} exhibits the extremal inputs on which the covering bound is
attained.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What was shown}

The empirical claim is narrow: two domains, two contexts, one model scale, identical
knees despite a twelve-point accuracy gap. The theory explains why this is not a
coincidence. (i) The sweep is a symmetric function of the demand multiset; the
correctness column is not an input. (ii) The joint map to (knee, accuracy) is
surjective, so no inequality links them. (iii) The knee is the $\lceil gn\rceil$-th
order statistic of the demand distribution while accuracy is a mean of a different
variable: order statistics ignore the bulk, means ignore the rank. (iv) Perturbation
robustness quantifies the slack: the demands of up to $(1-g)n - T_D(k)$ positions may
worsen arbitrarily without moving the knee.

Point (iv) is the honest mechanism: mathematical text plausibly does contain more
long-range references, but the fraction of positions depending on them stayed below
the gate slack. Difficulty concentrated in the mean; the quantile did not notice.

\subsection{Robustness and limitations}

Four objections are closed by theorems rather than argument. \emph{Gate tuning}: the
knees hold on entire admissible windows which overlap, so one gate certifies both
cells. \emph{Quality-scale artefacts}: the knee is invariant under any strictly
monotone distortion of the quality axis. \emph{Mixing ratio}: any prose--mathematics
mixture has knee $16$. \emph{Idealisation}: the measured step profile is realised
exactly by an honest workload.

Limitations are equally explicit. The mathematical corpus is classical mathematical
\emph{prose}, not modern notation-dense typeset mathematics. It is one corpus blend,
one model scale, two context lengths, and a fixed number of evaluation windows per
cell. The $+4$ increment is verified at $512 \to 1024$ only.

\subsection{Practical consequence}

A deployment table needs one row per \emph{offset}, not one per domain: two rows at
zero-to-three keys of tolerance, one row at four. Since the offset shifts rigidly with
scale and is invisible to difficulty, the configuration surface is far smaller than the
domain count suggests---tune per architecture and context, almost never per corpus.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Sub-additivity under corpus concatenation]
Mixing curves traps the knee between constituent knees; concatenating corpora adds the
demand multisets. For the union of workloads with position counts $n_1,n_2$,
\[ k^{*}(\text{union}, g) \le \max\bigl(k^{*}(\mathcal{D}_1,g_1),
k^{*}(\mathcal{D}_2,g_2)\bigr) \]
whenever $g \le (n_1 g_1 + n_2 g_2)/(n_1+n_2)$, with equality iff one demand multiset
dominates the other in the tail region.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Knee stability equals tail exchangeability]
Two domains have equal knees at every gate in a window iff their demand multisets agree
above the corresponding tail threshold.
\end{conjecture}

Empirically: modern notation-dense mathematics; non-English domains; whether the
increment remains $+4$ at context $4096$; and the three-domain table at substantially
larger model scale.

\section{Conclusion}

A truncated-memory system reading classical mathematics needs exactly as many keys as
one reading English prose---$16$ at context $512$, $20$ at context $1024$---while
predicting mathematics twelve percentage points worse. This is forced by the fact that
the two numbers are different species of statistic: accuracy is a mean of correctness,
the knee is the $\lceil gn\rceil$-th order statistic of demand, and the map to the pair
is surjective, so no law can bind them. The deployment consequence is a table with two
entries at tight tolerance and one entry at a tolerance of four keys---the exact
threshold, equal to one scale increment, at which packing and covering bounds over the
knee set $\{12,16\}$ agree on the answer $1$.

